<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2023</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sacred Ensembles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, inscribed in 2023. It comprises three temples of the 12th and 13th centuries: the Chennakeshava temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a>, the Hoysaleswara temple at Halebidu and the Chennakeshava temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somanathapura">Somanathapura</a>, the finest examples of Hoysala architecture and sculpture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacred-ensembles-of-the-hoysalas.jpg" alt="Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dineshkannambadi at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Somanathapura_Keshava_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
